From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abue Ammar <14371506+abue-ammar@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 06:16:38 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] Open the launcher, Notes, dialogs, Settings editors and menus
 with the native window bounce (#1643)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

* Open the launcher and Notes with the native window bounce

The palette and the Notes editor set animationBehavior to .none, so they
appeared instantly while Settings, AI Chat and Onboarding open with
AppKit's document-window animation. Both now use .documentWindow. The
note switcher and heading menu stay instant.

* Open dialogs and Settings editors with the native window bounce

DialogPanel and the Settings editor panel now use .documentWindow. This
replaces their custom 3pt slide and fade, the dialog's fadeOut, and the
editor's switch to .utilityWindow. Theme.DialogMotion goes with them.
The dim layers behind both keep their fades.

* Open palette menus with the native window bounce

MenuPanel now uses .documentWindow, so the ⌘K Actions menu, the menu
circle, header and input menus, and extension menus open and close with
AppKit's animation. The scale, settle and fade motion is gone, with
MenuPanelMotion, the extension's restated copy, the motion tokens, the
canvas geometry and its harness cases. The close animation keeps
drawing the panel after orderOut, so the hosted tree is released once
the panel's occlusion state reports it off screen.

* Open Camera, Quick Action, Notes popups and Dictation natively

The camera panels and the Quick Action panel drop their fade and SwiftUI
scale for .documentWindow. The Notes switcher, heading menu and the
Dictation pill had no animation and now use it too. A new
orderOutAnimated(then:) runs its callback once the close animation has
left the screen, so the camera still stops only after its panel is
gone; the menu panel's content release moves onto it.
